 "title": "N+1 queries",
 "sources": [
  "Django documentation, QuerySet API reference, select_related and prefetch_related (official, fetched 2026-11-17): without select_related, reading e.blog hits the database again to get the related Blog object; select_related joins the named foreign-key relationships in the same query, at the cost of a more complex initial query; it is limited to single-valued relationships (foreign key, one-to-one); prefetch_related does a separate lookup per relationship and joins in Python, allowing many-to-many and many-to-one, in one extra batch query each; Pizza example: a query on the Toppings table for every Pizza, reduced to two queries with prefetch_related. https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/stable/ref/models/querysets/#select-related",
  "Illustration, not a quoted case: 'a hundred entries' and '101 queries' are example numbers (1 list query + 1 per row), labelled on screen."
 ],
 "lines": [
  {
   "text": "One page load, and your database logs a hundred and one queries. That's the N plus one problem.",
   "caps": "*101 QUERIES | ONE PAGE LOAD | *N PLUS ONE"
  },
  {
   "text": "It starts with one query to fetch a list, say a hundred blog entries.",
   "caps": "*ONE QUERY | A LIST OF *100 ENTRIES"
  },
  {
   "text": "Then, in a loop, your code reads a related field on each entry, like its blog or its author.",
   "caps": "THEN A *LOOP | READ A *RELATED FIELD"
  },
  {
   "text": "With lazy loading, each of those reads quietly fires its own query, so a hundred entries means a hundred more.",
   "caps": "LAZY LOADING | *EACH ROW | *ITS OWN QUERY"
  },
  {
   "text": "That's one plus a hundred queries, when two would have done the job.",
   "caps": "*1 + 100 QUERIES | WHEN *TWO | WOULD DO"
  },
  {
   "text": "The fix is eager loading. In Django, select related joins the foreign key in the same query.",
   "caps": "*EAGER LOADING | *SELECT RELATED | *JOIN IT"
  },
  {
   "text": "But here's the catch: that only works for single-valued relationships, so many to many needs prefetch related, an extra query.",
   "caps": "BUT THERE IS A *CATCH | *SINGLE-VALUED ONLY | MANY-TO-MANY: *PREFETCH"
  },
  {
   "text": "So watch your query count in development, because the code looks innocent and the problem only shows up with real data.",
   "caps": "WATCH THE *QUERY COUNT | CODE LOOKS *INNOCENT | REAL DATA *EXPOSES IT"
  }
 ],
